# OpenStick Linux: GPU acceleration verdicts by graphics chip and driver (gpu.md "Policy").
# version: 2
#
# One line per rule, TAB-separated, first match wins, plain substrings (no patterns):
#
#   verdict  renderer-substring  driver-substring  mesa-prefix  min-GLES  note
#
# verdict      verified   tested by OpenStick on this chip AND driver: the recommended setting
#                         turns the GPU on by itself for Pro, once this device's own test passed.
#              beta       runs, or expected to, but not tested by OpenStick: one tap after a
#                         pass on this device, never automatic, not advertised.
#              blocked    known not to work: off, no override (a bad driver in the field reaches
#                         every device within a day through this file).
# renderer     a substring of the host's GL_RENDERER (the app reads it through EGL); empty = any.
# driver       a substring of the host's GL_VERSION, where the driver release is (r49p1); empty = any.
# mesa         the GUEST Mesa version (libgl1-mesa-dri) must start with this; empty = any.
# min-GLES     the host's OpenGL ES version the verdict needs; below it the device is blocked
#              (virglrenderer needs 3.1). Always set.
# note         shown to the user with the verdict.
#
# The app carries a copy in its assets; the higher "# version:" of the two wins, so a newer app
# can tighten this before apt does and apt can fix it before an app release. A "verified" line
# goes in only after the 2.0 soak (W12) on that device: zero crashes, flat memory.
#
# The order matters: a specific chip before its family, a family before the catch-alls.
